ong>Register</strong></td><td>frequent colloquial words</td><td>some informal words</td><td>mostly formal</td><td>consistently formal</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Precision</strong></td><td>no numbers/units</td><td>1 quantified detail</td><td>2–3 quantified details</td><td>quantified + compared / conditioned</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Objectivity</strong></td><td>emotive / overclaiming</td><td>some exaggeration</td><td>mostly evidence-based</td><td>evidence-based + appropriately hedged</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Clarity</strong></td><td>meaning often unclear</td><td>some unclear sentences</td><td>mostly clear</td><td>clear and controlled</td></tr></table></div><table class="score-table" style="margin-top:12px"><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Student self-score /4</th><th>Class review /4</th></tr><tr><td>Register</td><td>____</td><td>____</td></tr><tr><td>Precision</td><td>____</td><td>____</td></tr><tr><td>Objectivity</td><td>____</td><td>____</td></tr><tr><td>Clarity</td><td>____</td><td>____</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td><strong>____ /16</strong></td><td><strong>____ /16</strong></td></tr></table><div class="takeaway"><strong>Keep this score.</strong> Use the same four criteria later to measure progress rather than relying on impression alone.</div></section><section id="peer"><div class="head"><div><span class="label">98–108 minutes</span><h2>Peer Review Checklist</h2></div><p>Do not simply tick everything. Select <strong>Yes, Partly, or No</strong>, then give one strength and two actionable improvements.</p></div><div class="peer-wrap table-scroll"><table class="peer-table"><tr><th>Check</th><th>Yes</th><th>Partly</th><th>No</th></tr><tr><td>Avoids obviously <strong>colloquial expressions</strong>.</td><td><input aria-label="Colloquial expressions yes" name="p1" type="radio"/></td><td><input aria-label="Colloquial expressions partly" name="p1" type="radio"/></td><td><input aria-label="Colloquial expressions no" name="p1" type="radio"/></td></tr><tr><td>Uses <strong>objective</strong> rather than exaggerated language.</td><td><input aria-label="Objective language yes" name="p2" type="radio"/></td><td><input aria-label="Objective language partly" name="p2" type="radio"/></td><td><input aria-label="Objective language no" name="p2" type="radio"/></td></tr><tr><td>Includes <strong>precise quantities or technical details</strong> where possible.</td><td><input aria-label="Precision yes" name="p3" type="radio"/></td><td><input aria-label="Precision partly" name="p3" type="radio"/></td><td><input aria-label="Precision no" name="p3" type="radio"/></td></tr><tr><td>Uses <strong>accurate technical vocabulary</strong>.</td><td><input aria-label="Technical vocabulary yes" name="p4" type="radio"/></td><td><input aria-label="Technical vocabulary partly" name="p4" type="radio"/></td><td><input aria-label="Technical vocabulary no" name="p4" type="radio"/></td></tr><tr><td>Sentences are <strong>clear and controlled</strong>.</td><td><input aria-label="Sentence clarity yes" name="p5" type="radio"/></td><td><input aria-label="Sentence clarity partly" name="p5" type="radio"/></td><td><input aria-label="Sentence clarity no" name="p5" type="radio"/></td></tr><tr><td>The paragraph has <strong>one clear focus</strong>.</td><td><input aria-label="Focus yes" name="p6" type="radio"/></td><td><input aria-label="Focus partly" name="p6" type="radio"/></td><td><input aria-label="Focus no" name="p6" type="radio"/></td></tr></table></div><div class="grid" style="margin-top:12px"><div class="task"><h3>One Specific Strength</h3><input placeholder="The strongest feature is... because..." type="text"/></div><div class="task"><h3>Two Actionable Improvements</h3><textarea placeholder="1. ...
2. ..."></textarea></div></div></section><section id="reading"><div class="head"><div><span class="label">Extra reading</span><h2>Professional Academic English Resources</h2></div><p>Optional follow-up for students who want extra practice before later weeks.</p></div><div class="resource-grid-pro"><div class="resource-pro"><span class="priority">Start here</span><h3>University of Sydney — Academic Writing</h3><p>Explains academic writing as typically <strong>formal, objective and technical</strong>, with examples of formal vocabulary and cautious evaluation.</p><div class="why"><strong>Why read it:</strong> directly reinforces Week 1’s four core principles.</div><a href="https://www.sydney.edu.au/students/study-skills/writing.html" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Open University of Sydney resource →</a></div><div class="resource-pro"><span class="priority">Start here</span><h3>Monash University — Master Academic Language</h3><p>Practical guidance on <strong>formal, objective and impersonal</strong> academic language, including what to avoid in casual writing.</p><div class="why"><strong>Why read it:</strong> useful for register and tone practice.</div><a href="https://www.monash.edu/student-academic-success/excel-at-writing/improve-your-writing/master-academic-language" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Open Monash resource →</a></div><div class="resource-pro"><span class="res-tag">Clarity practice</span><h3>Monash University — Write Clear Sentences</h3><p>Focuses on writing that is <strong>clear, concise and precise</strong>, using accurate terminology without unnecessary complexity.</p><div class="why"><strong>Why read it:</strong> strong follow-up to the precision section.</div><a href="https://www.monash.edu/student-academic-success/excel-at-writing/improve-your-writing/write-clearly/write-clear-sentences" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Open sentence clarity resource →</a></div><div class="resource-pro"><span class="res-tag">Phrase practice</span><h3>University of Manchester — Academic Phrasebank</h3><p>A large bank of academic language organised by functions such as <strong>defining terms, describing quantities, comparing, explaining causality and signalling transitions</strong>.</p><div class="why"><strong>Why read it:</strong> useful throughout the entire course, especially Weeks 5, 9, 12 and 13.</div><a href="https://www.phrasebank.manchester.ac.uk/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Open Academic Phrasebank →</a></div><div class="resource-pro"><span class="res-tag">Sentence control</span><h3>Purdue OWL — Sentence Clarity</h3><p>Explains why <strong>clear sentence structure</strong> matters for communication, credibility and reader understanding.</p><div class="why"><strong>Why